    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-43295",
      "knowledge_point_id": "240a0a0b-fb86-5760-bb2b-74275708ff77",
      "knowledge_point": "Simplifying an Expression Using the Alternate Forms of the Cotangent-Cosecant Identity",
      "problem": "Simplify $\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta -1}-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +1}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we put both fractions over a common denominator and then subtract, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta -1}-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +1} & =\\frac{1\\cdot (\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +1)}{(\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta -1)(\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +1)}-\\frac{1\\cdot (\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta -1)}{(\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta -1)(\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +1)-(\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta -1)}{(\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta -1)(\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +1-\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +1}{{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta -1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta -1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the identity ${csc}^{2}⁡\\theta -1={cot}^{2}⁡\\theta ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta -1} & =\\frac{2}{{cot}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =2{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta -1}-\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta +1}=2{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
